ere.\n\nTherefore, I'm folding.\n\n\n", 'output': "Evaluation:\nThe current board is 2 of Hearts, 3 of Spades, 8 of Diamonds, 10 of Clubs, 10 of Diamonds. You hold the King of Clubs and 5 of Spades. This gives you a final hand of a Pair of 10s (from the board) with a King kicker (K, 10, 10, 8, 5).\n\nThe game was checked down (no betting) through the Flop and Turn, indicating weakness from both sides. However, on the River, the opponent suddenly bet 740. Since the pot was small (likely just blinds), this is a significant bet. While the opponent could be bluffing to steal the pot after seeing weakness, your hand is very vulnerable. You lose to any player holding an Ace (A-high beats K-high), any pocket pair, or any card matching the board (2, 3, 8, or 10). You only beat a pure bluff with Queen-high or lower. Given that you have a chip lead and a weak hand, there is no incentive to make a risky hero call here.\n\nReason:\nYour hand (King-high relative to the board's pair) is too weak to justify calling a large river bet. The opponent's sudden aggression suggests they likely have at least a pair or Ace-high, both of which beat you. Preserving your chip lead is more important than catching a potential bluff in a low-value pot.\n\nAnswer:\n-1"}
