n is -1.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: I am currently in the Small Blind with Ace-Six offsuit (A6o). While A6o is a decent heads-up hand that typically warrants a raise or a call, the tournament situation is the overriding factor. I am leading by a cumulative score of 835 chips with only 4 hands remaining in the match (Hands 17, 18, 19, 20). The blinds are 50/100. The total cost to fold every remaining hand is 300 chips (Hand 17: -50, Hand 18: -100, Hand 19: -50, Hand 20: -100). Since my lead of 835 chips is significantly larger than the maximum loss from folding out (300 chips), I can guarantee a win by simply folding every hand. Playing the hand introduces the risk of a "bad beat" or a large loss that could jeopardize the lead. Therefore, the strategy that maximizes the probability of winning the game to 100% is to take the path of zero variance.\n\nReason: To maximize the probability of winning the game, I should avoid all variance. With a lead of 835 and remaining "blind out" cost of only 300, folding guarantees the victory, whereas playing opens the door to potential losses.\n\nAnswer: -1'}
